{# ==========================================================================
     05 · Speed
     The two charts and the tables under them are measured, from
     github.com/phplrt/benchmarks — absolute values, in µs, ms and kb.

     The "how much work do you need?" panel is deliberately NOT absolute. It
     shows three percentages of a full parse, and its caveat box says so. If
     microseconds ever go back in there, the caveat has to go back too.
     ====================================================================== #}
<section class="band band--sunk" id="speed">
    <div class="hold">
        <div class="head rise">
            <span class="chapter">05 · speed</span>
            <h2 class="title">Ten times the input, ten times the time</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                On a short type every parser here takes microseconds, and the
                hand-written ones are ahead: <code>vimeo/psalm</code> reads
                <code>array&lt;int, User&gt;</code> in 6.2 µs, <code>phpstan/phpdoc-parser</code>
                in 16, phplrt in 19. From a kilobyte on phplrt is the fastest of the five,
                and the gap keeps opening: phplrt takes ×9.9 longer for every ×10 more
                input, which is about as close to linear as parsing gets, while
                <code>phpstan/phpdoc-parser</code> takes ×37 longer. On 64 KB that is
                33 ms against 425 ms.
            </p>
        </div>

        {#
          COORDINATE PLAN — shared by both charts on this page.

          Grid (1000 × 500). Plot box x 96…800, y 30…430.

            x = 96 + log4(bytes / 16) × 117.33
                16 B = 96      64 B = 213.3   256 B = 330.7   1 KB = 448
                4 KB = 565.3   16 KB = 682.7  64 KB = 800

          Every sample is ×4 the one before (16 B → 64 KB is 4⁶), so on a log
          axis the seven of them stand exactly 117.33px apart and the ticks
          land where the data does.

          Time, six decades over 400px (1 µs … 1 s):
            y = 430 − (log10(ms) + 3) × 66.667
                10 µs = 363.3   100 µs = 296.7   1 ms = 230
                10 ms = 163.3   100 ms = 96.7    1 s  = 30

          Memory, five decades over 400px (1 kb … 100 000 kb, 1 kb = 1024 bytes):
            y = 430 − log10(kb) × 80
                10 kb = 350   100 kb = 270   1 000 kb = 190
                10 000 kb = 110   100 000 kb = 30

          Every point below is that formula applied to the table in the same
          panel; nothing here is eyeballed.
        #}
